WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:06.580
Moments ago, you made a decision to click
on this video.

00:00:06.580 --> 00:00:10.410
Maybe YouTube has been gently poking you with
suggestions to watch this.

00:00:10.410 --> 00:00:12.249
Or maybe it’s part of your routine.

00:00:12.249 --> 00:00:17.120
Whatever the reason, we hope the decision
was an easy one to make.

00:00:17.120 --> 00:00:22.820
But even if the decision was easy, the steps
it took to execute that choice, the information

00:00:22.820 --> 00:00:26.539
you needed to gather, were not simple.

00:00:26.539 --> 00:00:31.210
From reading the title to physically clicking
the link to settling back into a comfortable

00:00:31.210 --> 00:00:37.360
position, your body had to coordinate a ton
of processes using chemical and electrical

00:00:37.360 --> 00:00:41.649
signals to quickly send instructions all over
the place.

00:00:41.649 --> 00:00:50.760
These signals are rooted in your nervous system,
a reflection of your multicellular complexity.

00:00:50.760 --> 00:00:55.539
Life in the microcosmos comes with its own
choices though.

00:00:55.539 --> 00:01:00.739
And while they may not have to coordinate
multiple limbs, single-celled eukaryotes,

00:01:00.739 --> 00:01:04.390
or protists, still have decisions to make.

00:01:04.390 --> 00:01:10.530
They've got places to be, things to do--and
they don't have a brain to help them.

00:01:10.530 --> 00:01:16.960
All they've got is the one cell to deal with
all of life's complications.

00:01:16.960 --> 00:01:21.640
Microbiologists have been fascinated by the
way protists respond to their environment

00:01:21.640 --> 00:01:23.370
for more than a century.

00:01:23.370 --> 00:01:31.340
And so naturally, that has led some scientists
to try and provoke a response, like James--our

00:01:31.340 --> 00:01:36.369
master of microscopes--using a piece of hair
to poke these stentors.

00:01:36.369 --> 00:01:46.009
In 1906, a zoologist named Herbert Spencer
Jennings published Behavior of the Lower Organisms,

00:01:46.009 --> 00:01:55.009
a 366 page book containing sections with titles
like "The Daily Life of Paramecium" and "Reactions

00:01:55.009 --> 00:01:58.790
of Infusoria to the Electrical Current".

00:01:58.790 --> 00:02:04.860
Nestled among descriptions of the many organisms
and behaviors he observed was a section documenting

00:02:04.860 --> 00:02:10.750
the way stentors responded when he injected
carmine into the water around them.

00:02:10.750 --> 00:02:15.910
Carmine is a red dye and apparently it’s
something that Stentors don’t like very much.

00:02:15.910 --> 00:02:22.730
Like our stentors, the subjects of Jennings'
experiment did not particularly revel in this irritant.

00:02:22.730 --> 00:02:28.360
In our case, you can see the stentors trying
to act like everything is normal.

00:02:28.360 --> 00:02:34.870
But with repeated poking, they start to contract,
evading the troublesome hair but also missing

00:02:34.870 --> 00:02:38.290
out on opportunities to gather passing food.

00:02:38.290 --> 00:02:43.200
They stay contracted for a short period, but
eventually they emerge and extend back to

00:02:43.200 --> 00:02:46.550
their original length.

00:02:46.550 --> 00:02:51.060
In Jennings' case, the stentors also attempted
to ride out this irritant.

00:02:51.060 --> 00:02:57.690
But when that failed, those organisms then
began to contort and twist to the side.

00:02:57.690 --> 00:03:02.670
And in that way, the stentors could attempt
to evade the carmine while still focusing

00:03:02.670 --> 00:03:05.400
on what's truly important: food.

00:03:08.600 --> 00:03:12.440
This avoidance reaction is a common one among
protists.

00:03:12.440 --> 00:03:18.250
In the same text, Jennings describes the tendency
of some organisms to avoid light, similar

00:03:18.250 --> 00:03:20.750
to this trachelophyllum here.

00:03:20.750 --> 00:03:25.360
Those changes in brightness that you see are
coming from the microscope as the diaphragm

00:03:25.360 --> 00:03:30.040
on the condenser opens up, allowing more light
to enter the slide.

00:03:30.040 --> 00:03:35.760
As the slide quickly gets brighter, you can
see the trachelophyllum trying to swim away.

00:03:38.740 --> 00:03:44.140
This organism isn't that well-studied, so
we don't know exactly why it's trying to avoid

00:03:44.150 --> 00:03:45.310
the light.

00:03:45.310 --> 00:03:51.090
Some microbes definitely seek out dark areas
to avoid predators, so that's a possible explanation.

00:03:51.090 --> 00:03:54.610
The light itself may also be harmful to the
organism.

00:03:54.610 --> 00:03:59.980
Whatever the reason, this trachelophyllum
isn't alone among protists.

00:03:59.980 --> 00:04:05.480
One of Stentor coeruleus' many compelling
traits is its photosensitivity.

00:04:05.480 --> 00:04:11.230
Inside its unicellular, trumpet-shaped body
are photosensitive pigment granules called

00:04:11.230 --> 00:04:18.070
stentorin that, in the presence of light,
drive electrical changes in the plasma membrane

00:04:18.070 --> 00:04:23.980
and reverse the movements of its cilia, transporting
the organism away.

00:04:23.980 --> 00:04:29.850
Of course, there are also protists that seek
out light, like the photosynthetic Euglena

00:04:29.850 --> 00:04:33.230
that rely on sunlight to make their food.

00:04:33.230 --> 00:04:39.230
Even then, there are subtleties in their response,
which Jennings observed and described.

00:04:39.230 --> 00:04:44.280
While the Euglena in his experiments sought
out light as expected, if the light was too

00:04:44.280 --> 00:04:49.710
strong or brought on too suddenly, the euglena
sought shadier refuge.

00:04:49.710 --> 00:04:55.210
Another protist that captured Jennings' attention
was the paramecium, that seemingly simple

00:04:55.210 --> 00:05:00.400
organism that has captivated many scientists
because of its hidden complexities.

00:05:00.400 --> 00:05:06.160
Here, you can see the paramecium on this slide
in their own series of avoidance reactions.

00:05:06.160 --> 00:05:10.260
And the main thing they seem to be trying
to avoid is each other.

00:05:10.260 --> 00:05:14.920
That might sound fairly familiar to some of
us these days.

00:05:14.920 --> 00:05:20.410
As they navigate the crowd, you can see the
paramecium actually turn around, which is

00:05:20.410 --> 00:05:23.560
coordinated by the movement of their cilia.

00:05:23.560 --> 00:05:29.160
By changing the direction that the cilia move
in, the organism is able to reverse their

00:05:29.160 --> 00:05:30.160
direction.

00:05:30.160 --> 00:05:33.570
They can also spin, turn, or just come to
a stop.

00:05:33.570 --> 00:05:38.360
If they do a number of these reactions in
quick succession, the paramecium can even

00:05:38.360 --> 00:05:40.770
swim backwards.

00:05:40.770 --> 00:05:48.250
These movements are a joy to watch, and it
is astounding to consider how a unicellular

00:05:48.250 --> 00:05:52.690
organism is able to change its behavior based
on its surroundings.

00:05:52.690 --> 00:05:59.120
But Jennings' findings extended beyond just
the notion that an organism will react to

00:05:59.120 --> 00:06:00.580
its surroundings.

00:06:00.580 --> 00:06:06.020
As he continued to prod his stentors with
carmine powder, he noticed that the organism

00:06:06.020 --> 00:06:11.360
began to explore other strategies to deal
with the irritant.

00:06:11.360 --> 00:06:16.920
When avoiding the carmine failed to stop the
provocation, the organism would temporarily

00:06:16.920 --> 00:06:22.020
reverse the movement of its cilia, driving
the water current away from the stentor so

00:06:22.020 --> 00:06:27.300
it could essentially "spit" water out of its
mouth.

00:06:27.300 --> 00:06:32.100
And when that didn't work, the stentor tried
to contract away from the stimulus.

00:06:32.100 --> 00:06:37.610
And when that didn't work, well, then the
stentor just up and left, detaching themselves

00:06:37.610 --> 00:06:44.040
from their substrate and swimming away in
search of a quieter, less carmine-filled home.

00:06:44.040 --> 00:06:50.620
There's a pretty key implication to Jennings'
observations here: the stentors he observed

00:06:50.620 --> 00:06:57.560
didn't just react to stimulus, they worked
through a hierarchy of possible responses.

00:06:57.560 --> 00:07:02.820
Think of it the way a cat deals with the common
irritant of a loving human who wants to cuddle:

00:07:02.820 --> 00:07:08.300
they start with simple evasion tactics, but
they might then escalate to swiping and hissing

00:07:08.300 --> 00:07:12.510
before just picking up and running to a corner
where they can't be bothered.

00:07:12.510 --> 00:07:18.880
In the case of Jennings' stentors, which fortunately
did not have sharp claws, there seemed to

00:07:18.880 --> 00:07:23.040
be a ranked preference to the measures they
were going to take.

00:07:23.040 --> 00:07:28.930
There was just one problem with Jennings'
results: no one could replicate them.

00:07:28.930 --> 00:07:34.540
And so for more than a century, his observations
were set aside until a scientist named Jeremy

00:07:34.540 --> 00:07:40.560
Gunawardena decided to dig deeper and found
that these follow-up studies used a different

00:07:40.560 --> 00:07:44.010
Stentor species than Jennings had used.

00:07:44.010 --> 00:07:49.520
When Gunawardena and his team gathered the
original species, Stentor roeselii, they found

00:07:49.520 --> 00:07:53.970
that their subjects exhibited those same behaviors.

00:07:53.970 --> 00:07:59.669
Interestingly though, different specimens
of the same species seemed to have their own

00:07:59.669 --> 00:08:02.070
ranked preferences.

00:08:02.070 --> 00:08:08.210
Some might only contract, while others would
perform a mix of bending and cilia-reversal.

00:08:08.210 --> 00:08:13.090
We think the stentors we've been poking here
are Stentor roeselii.

00:08:13.090 --> 00:08:18.889
And if they are, they seem to have preferred
the contraction approach themselves, until

00:08:18.889 --> 00:08:20.900
they gave in and swam away.

00:08:25.290 --> 00:08:30.120
For the past few minutes, we've been following
one of them as it tries to find a new home,

00:08:30.120 --> 00:08:34.010
brushing up against possible substrates with
its oral cilia.

00:08:34.010 --> 00:08:38.520
We think this is part of their inspection
process, and if they don't like what they

00:08:38.520 --> 00:08:42.200
feel, they swim backwards and away.

00:08:42.200 --> 00:08:47.270
This little guy has tried a few locations,
but here, it seems to have found something

00:08:47.270 --> 00:08:48.800
intriguing.

00:08:48.800 --> 00:08:54.940
Even as other stentors come in and interfere,
this one persists, returning to inspect its

00:08:54.940 --> 00:09:01.390
newfound spot again and again like it’s
about to make an expensive purchase.

00:09:01.390 --> 00:09:06.370
And when it's satisfied with its decision,
the stentor releases an adhesive substance

00:09:06.370 --> 00:09:10.800
and moves in, ready to eat until the next
decision needs to be made.

00:09:11.780 --> 00:09:14.760
And if that isn’t life, I don’t know what
is.

00:09:17.520 --> 00:09:23.940
It is remarkable to think that just choosing
the wrong species of Stentor can give such

00:09:23.950 --> 00:09:30.230
different results, that decision-making--even
for the simplest of organisms--is such a complicated

00:09:30.230 --> 00:09:33.900
endeavor for us to untangle.

00:09:33.900 --> 00:09:40.730
We're still only at the early stages of understanding
what these actions and reactions require from

00:09:40.730 --> 00:09:47.310
different organisms, and for some, it is likely
to be much simpler than for others.

00:09:47.310 --> 00:09:53.000
What's most remarkable, as always seems the
case with the microcosmos, is just how many

00:09:53.000 --> 00:09:59.300
ways there are to do anything at all.

00:09:59.300 --> 00:10:04.740
Thank you for coming on this journey with
us as we explore the unseen world that surrounds us.

00:10:04.740 --> 00:10:08.640
If you liked it, the people on screen now
are some of the people you should be thanking

00:10:08.640 --> 00:10:11.540
because they are our patrons on Patreon.

00:10:11.540 --> 00:10:14.850
And they allow us to do this wonderful, weird
thing.

00:10:14.850 --> 00:10:18.890
If you want to be one of those people, you
can go to patreon.com/journeytomicro.

00:10:18.890 --> 00:10:22.080
We would really appreciate it.

00:10:22.080 --> 00:10:24.149
And thank you to everybody who supports us.

